In this lesson we'll look at how relative frequency can help us estimate probability when an experiment is just too tricky to model exactly. So here's the big idea. You run the experiment a bunch of times, and you keep track of how often the event you're interested in actually happens. Then you divide that count by the total number of trials you ran. That fraction is your relative frequency, and it gives you a rough estimate of the true probability. Now here's the really important part. When you only do a few trials, the number can jump around a lot from one run to the next, so don't panic if it looks all over the place. But as you crank up the number of trials, the relative frequency tends to settle down and cluster closer and closer to the real probability. Think of it like the difference between flipping a coin five times versus five hundred times. The big picture gets clearer the more data you collect. Now let's see it in action.